(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[27] = {"n":27,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 20% · B2 50% · B2+ 30%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Superconductors","p":["Every time electricity flows through an ordinary wire, some of its energy is lost as heat. This happens because the electrons that carry the current collide with the atoms of the metal, which resist their movement. In power lines, these losses amount to a significant share of all the electricity generated. Superconductors are materials that, under certain conditions, conduct electricity with no resistance at all, allowing a current to flow indefinitely without losing energy.","Superconductivity was discovered by accident in 1911 by the Dutch physicist Heike Kamerlingh Onnes. He had recently succeeded in liquefying helium, which made it possible to cool materials to just a few degrees above absolute zero, the lowest possible temperature, equal to minus 273 degrees Celsius. When he cooled mercury to about minus 269 degrees, he found that its electrical resistance suddenly disappeared completely. At first, he suspected a fault in his equipment, but repeated experiments confirmed the result.","Superconductors have another remarkable property. In 1933, two German physicists discovered that superconductors expel magnetic fields from their interior, an effect now named after one of them, Walther Meissner. As a result, a magnet placed above a cooled superconductor can float in the air, a striking demonstration often shown in science museums. Explaining why superconductivity occurs proved extremely difficult. A widely accepted theory for conventional superconductors was not developed until 1957, when three American physicists showed that, at very low temperatures, electrons can form pairs that move through the material without being scattered.","For decades, the main obstacle to practical use was temperature. Early superconductors had to be cooled with liquid helium, which is expensive and difficult to handle. In 1986, however, researchers discovered a family of ceramic materials that became superconducting at much higher temperatures. Some of these 'high-temperature superconductors' work above minus 196 degrees Celsius, the temperature of liquid nitrogen, which is far cheaper than helium. The discovery caused great excitement, though even these materials still require considerable cooling, and scientists still do not fully understand how they work.","Superconductors already have important applications. The most familiar is magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI, used in hospitals to produce detailed images of the inside of the body. MRI scanners rely on powerful superconducting magnets. Superconducting magnets are also used in particle accelerators, such as the Large Hadron Collider in Switzerland, and in some experimental high-speed trains that float above the track using magnetic levitation, eliminating friction with the rails. Researchers are also developing superconducting cables that could carry electricity through cities with minimal losses.","The ultimate goal is a material that is superconducting at room temperature and normal pressure. Such a discovery could transform energy transmission, transport and computing. In recent years, several research groups have announced room-temperature superconductors, attracting worldwide attention, but these claims have either required enormous pressures or could not be confirmed by other scientists. The search continues, and it remains one of the most exciting challenges in physics. Until it succeeds, the extreme cold required by superconductors will continue to limit their use."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how electricity is generated in power stations","the life of Heike Kamerlingh Onnes","how hospitals use X-rays","the properties, history and uses of superconductors"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers these topics.","h":"الموصلات الفائقة."},{"n":2,"s":"Why is energy lost in ordinary wires?","o":["The wires are too cold.","Magnetic fields block the current.","Electrons collide with atoms of the metal.","The wires are made of ceramic."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"تصادم الإلكترونات."},{"n":3,"s":"What made Kamerlingh Onnes's discovery possible?","o":["his success in liquefying helium","the invention of MRI scanners","the discovery of ceramic materials","a theory developed in 1957"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Liquid helium allowed extreme cooling.","h":"تسييل الهيليوم."},{"n":4,"s":"What can be inferred from the fact that Onnes first suspected a fault in his equipment?","o":["His equipment was usually unreliable.","The result was very unexpected.","He had predicted the result.","Other scientists had already discovered it."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"He did not expect resistance to vanish.","h":"نتيجة غير متوقعة."},{"n":5,"s":"The word 'expel' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["push out","absorb","create","strengthen"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Superconductors push magnetic fields out.","h":"يطرد."},{"n":6,"s":"Why was the discovery of 1986 important?","o":["They worked at room temperature.","They needed no cooling at all.","They explained all superconductivity.","The new materials worked at higher temperatures that were cheaper to reach."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"درجات حرارة أعلى وأرخص."},{"n":7,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'they' in 'how they work' refers to","o":["scientists","liquid nitrogen","early superconductors","high-temperature superconductors"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"Scientists do not understand how these materials work.","h":"تعود على الموصلات عالية الحرارة."},{"n":8,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an application of superconductors?","o":["MRI scanners","mobile phone batteries","particle accelerators","magnetic levitation trains"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Phone batteries are not mentioned.","h":"بطاريات الهواتف غير مذكورة."},{"n":9,"s":"What does the writer suggest about recent claims of room-temperature superconductors?","o":["They have been widely accepted.","They are already used in power lines.","They have not been convincingly confirmed.","They were made in 1911."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"They required huge pressures or could not be confirmed.","h":"لم تُؤكد."},{"n":10,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the passage?","o":["Superconductors have no practical applications.","Superconductors are very useful, but the need for extreme cold limits their use.","Room-temperature superconductors are now common.","Superconductors waste more energy than ordinary wires."],"k":1,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It summarizes the main points.","h":"مفيدة لكنها تحتاج تبريدًا شديدًا."}]},{"t":"Roman roads","p":["The saying 'all roads lead to Rome' reflects one of the greatest achievements of the ancient world. At its height, the Roman Empire was connected by a network of more than 80,000 kilometres of paved roads, along with many more kilometres of smaller tracks. These roads stretched from Britain to Egypt and from Spain to Mesopotamia, and many of them remained in use for centuries after the empire itself had disappeared.","The first great Roman road was the Via Appia, begun in 312 BCE. It originally linked Rome with the city of Capua to the south, and it was later extended to the port of Brundisium, the gateway to Greece and the East. Its main purpose was military: Rome was expanding its control over Italy, and it needed to move soldiers and supplies quickly. This remained a key reason for road building throughout Roman history. A legion could march around thirty kilometres a day on a good road, far faster than across open country.","Roman engineers were remarkably skilled. Wherever possible, they built roads in straight lines, cutting through hills and building bridges and causeways across rivers and marshes. A typical major road was constructed in several layers. Workers first dug a trench and filled it with large stones, then added layers of smaller stones, gravel and sand mixed with lime. The surface was often paved with large, closely fitted stone blocks. Roads were slightly curved, higher in the middle than at the edges, so that rainwater drained into ditches at the sides. This careful construction explains why sections of Roman roads survive even today.","The roads served many purposes beyond the military. Officials and messengers of the imperial postal service, the cursus publicus, used them to carry government orders across the empire. Along the main routes, stations were built at regular intervals where travellers could change horses, find food and spend the night. Milestones recorded distances to the nearest towns, and some travellers carried itineraries listing the stations along their route, a kind of ancient road map. Merchants used the roads too, although transporting heavy goods by land remained slow and expensive, so the sea and rivers were preferred for bulk cargo.","Roman roads also extended into Arabia. After the Romans annexed the Nabataean kingdom in 106 CE, creating the province of Arabia Petraea, Emperor Trajan ordered the construction of the Via Nova Traiana, a road running from Bostra in southern Syria to Aila, on the Red Sea, near modern Aqaba. The road helped to control the region and to protect the lucrative trade routes that brought incense and spices from southern Arabia.","The roads had important cultural consequences as well. By making travel easier, they helped to spread the Latin language, Roman law and new ideas throughout the empire. However, they could also be used by invading armies and helped to spread diseases. After the collapse of the Western Roman Empire in the fifth century, many roads were poorly maintained, and stones were taken for other buildings. Even so, the routes of many modern European highways still follow the paths laid out by Roman engineers two thousand years ago."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Fall of the Roman Empire","The Roads That Held an Empire Together","How to Build a Modern Highway","The History of the Red Sea"],"k":1,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage focuses on Roman roads.","h":"طرق الإمبراطورية."},{"n":12,"s":"What was the main original purpose of the Via Appia?","o":["to carry incense from Arabia","to connect Rome with Britain","to help farmers sell their crops","to move soldiers and supplies quickly"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"غرض عسكري."},{"n":13,"s":"Why were Roman roads curved, higher in the middle than at the edges?","o":["to make them look more attractive","so that rainwater would drain away","to slow down horses","to save building materials"],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"تصريف مياه الأمطار."},{"n":14,"s":"What can be inferred from the fact that sections of Roman roads survive today?","o":["They were built very recently.","They were rarely used.","They were built with great care and skill.","They were made of wood."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 3 links survival to construction.","h":"دقة البناء."},{"n":15,"s":"What was the cursus publicus?","o":["the imperial postal service","a type of road surface","a Roman legion","a trading company"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines it.","h":"خدمة البريد."},{"n":16,"s":"Why did merchants prefer the sea and rivers for heavy cargo?","o":["Roads were closed to merchants.","The sea was always safer.","Roads did not reach any ports.","Land transport of heavy goods was slow and expensive."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"النقل البري بطيء ومكلف."},{"n":17,"s":"The word 'lucrative' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["dangerous","ancient","profitable","narrow"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Trade routes that made a lot of money.","h":"مربح."},{"n":18,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Via Nova Traiana?","o":["to describe the longest Roman road","to explain why Rome collapsed","to show that Roman roads extended into Arabia","to compare Roman and Nabataean art"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"It is an example in Arabia.","h":"امتداد الطرق إلى الجزيرة العربية."},{"n":19,"s":"In paragraph 6, the word 'they' in 'they could also be used by invading armies' refers to","o":["the Romans","new ideas","diseases","the roads"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"The roads could be used by invaders.","h":"تعود على الطرق."},{"n":20,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of Roman roads?","o":["the invention of the wheel","the spread of the Latin language","the spread of Roman law","the spread of diseases"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"The wheel is not mentioned.","h":"العجلة غير مذكورة."}]},{"t":"Synesthesia","p":["For most people, the senses work separately: we see colours with our eyes, hear sounds with our ears and taste food with our tongues. For some people, however, stimulating one sense automatically produces an experience in another. A person might see colours when listening to music, or taste particular flavours when hearing certain words. This unusual condition is called synesthesia, from Greek words meaning 'together' and 'sensation'.","There are many different forms of synesthesia. One of the most common is grapheme-colour synesthesia, in which letters or numbers are perceived as having specific colours. For such a person, the letter A might always appear red, while the number 7 is always green, even when they are printed in black ink. In another form, sounds trigger the experience of colours or shapes, so that a piano note might produce a flash of blue. Some synesthetes experience days of the week or months of the year as having particular positions in space around their bodies.","For many years, scientists were sceptical about synesthesia, suspecting that people were simply describing memories or using metaphors. Modern research, however, has shown that it is a genuine and consistent phenomenon. In one test, people are asked to choose colours for a long list of letters and then tested again, without warning, months later. Synesthetes typically choose almost exactly the same colours, whereas non-synesthetes who try to remember their earlier choices perform much worse. Brain imaging studies have also shown that, in grapheme-colour synesthetes, areas involved in processing colour become active when they look at black letters.","Estimates of how common synesthesia is vary, but recent studies suggest that it affects around four per cent of the population, far more than was once believed. It tends to run in families, which suggests a genetic component, although members of the same family may experience different types. Interestingly, the specific associations may also be influenced by experience: one study found that a number of synesthetes born in the 1970s had colours for letters that matched a popular set of coloured magnetic letters sold during their childhood.","Why does synesthesia occur? One leading theory proposes that it results from extra connections between neighbouring areas of the brain. In infancy, the brain has many more connections than it needs, and many are later removed in a process called pruning. In synesthetes, some of these connections may survive. Another theory suggests that the connections are present in everyone, but in synesthetes, the normal mechanisms that keep sensory information separate are weaker.","Synesthesia is generally not considered a disorder. Most synesthetes find their experiences pleasant or neutral, and many are surprised to learn that other people do not share them. Some research suggests that synesthetes may have advantages in certain types of memory; the colours associated with numbers, for example, can make phone numbers easier to remember. Synesthesia appears to be more common among artists, musicians and writers, and several famous painters and composers have described synesthetic experiences. Studying synesthesia helps scientists understand how the brain combines information from different senses to create our experience of the world."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["a condition in which one sense triggers another","how the eyes detect colour","the history of Greek words","how to improve memory"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains synesthesia.","h":"الحس المرافق."},{"n":22,"s":"In grapheme-colour synesthesia, what happens?","o":["Sounds are perceived as tastes.","Colours cannot be seen at all.","Words are perceived as shapes in space.","Letters or numbers are perceived as having colours."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"الحروف ملونة."},{"n":23,"s":"Why were scientists once sceptical about synesthesia?","o":["They had no instruments to measure colour.","Synesthetes refused to be studied.","They thought people might be describing memories or metaphors.","It was considered a dangerous illness."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"ظنوها ذكريات أو مجازًا."},{"n":24,"s":"What did the repeated colour test show?","o":["Synesthetes forgot their earlier choices.","Synesthetes chose almost the same colours months later.","Non-synesthetes performed better.","Nobody remembered the colours."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 describes the result.","h":"ثبات الاختيارات."},{"n":25,"s":"The word 'consistent' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["imaginary","rare","staying the same over time","frightening"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The experiences remain the same.","h":"ثابت."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the study of magnetic letters suggest?","o":["Childhood experiences may influence specific associations.","Synesthesia is caused only by toys.","Synesthesia has no genetic basis.","Magnetic letters damage the brain."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Colours matched a popular toy set.","h":"أثر التجارب في الطفولة."},{"n":27,"s":"According to one theory, why do synesthetes have extra connections in the brain?","o":["They use their brains more than others.","Some connections that are usually removed in infancy survive.","They are born with larger brains.","They learn to create new connections."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains pruning.","h":"بقاء الوصلات."},{"n":28,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'many' in 'many are later removed' refers to","o":["infants","theories","synesthetes","connections"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"Many connections are removed.","h":"تعود على الوصلات."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage?","o":["Synesthesia can be cured with medicine.","Synesthesia tends to run in families.","It may help some types of memory.","It appears more common among artists."],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"No cure is mentioned; it is not a disorder.","h":"العلاج غير مذكور."},{"n":30,"s":"How would you describe the writer's attitude towards synesthesia?","o":["interested and objective","alarmed and worried","dismissive","critical of synesthetes"],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The writer explains with interest.","h":"مهتم وموضوعي."}]},{"t":"The food-miles debate","p":["Walk into a supermarket almost anywhere in the world and you will find food that has travelled remarkable distances: apples from New Zealand, avocados from Mexico, rice from Thailand and berries from Chile. The term 'food miles' was introduced in the 1990s to describe the distance food travels from where it is produced to where it is eaten. Campaigners argued that buying local food would reduce the greenhouse gases produced by transport and help to fight climate change.","The idea is intuitively appealing. Transporting food by lorry, ship and especially aeroplane requires fuel, and it seems obvious that food grown nearby must have a smaller environmental impact. Buying local food also has other attractions: it can support local farmers, strengthen rural communities and provide fresher produce. In many countries, farmers' markets and 'buy local' campaigns have become increasingly popular, and some shops now label products with the distance they have travelled.","However, researchers who have studied the full life cycle of food products have found that the picture is more complicated. For most foods, transport accounts for only a small proportion of their total greenhouse gas emissions, often less than ten per cent. Far more emissions come from production itself: the use of fertilizers, the energy used in farming and processing, and, above all, the methane produced by cattle and sheep. As a result, what we eat usually matters much more than where it comes from.","A well-known study compared tomatoes grown in Britain with tomatoes imported from Spain. Because British tomatoes were often grown in heated greenhouses during the colder months, they were responsible for more emissions than Spanish tomatoes grown outdoors in the sun and transported by lorry. Similarly, studies have suggested that lamb raised on grass in New Zealand and shipped to Britain may, in some cases, have a lower carbon footprint than lamb raised in Britain, where animals may require more feed and energy.","The method of transport also makes a big difference. Shipping by sea is extremely efficient per kilogram of food, while air freight produces dozens of times more emissions per kilogram than sea transport. Air-freighted foods, such as some fresh fruits, vegetables and fish, represent only a tiny share of the food we eat but a disproportionate share of transport emissions. Interestingly, the final stage of the journey can also be significant: a single car trip to the shops to buy a small amount of food may produce more emissions per item than the food's long journey by ship.","None of this means that local food has no value. In regions such as the Gulf, where most food is imported, increasing local production can improve food security and reduce dependence on global supply chains, as recent disruptions have shown. Saudi Arabia, for example, has invested in modern greenhouses and hydroponic farms that use less water. The lesson of the food-miles debate is not that distance is irrelevant, but that simple rules can be misleading. Reducing the environmental impact of our diets requires attention to the whole system, from the farm to the plate."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how to grow tomatoes in greenhouses","whether the distance food travels is a good measure of its environmental impact","the history of supermarkets","why New Zealand exports lamb"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage examines the food-miles idea.","h":"جدل أميال الغذاء."},{"n":32,"s":"Why did campaigners encourage people to buy local food?","o":["to make food cheaper","to increase imports","to reduce greenhouse gases from transport","to help supermarkets"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"تقليل انبعاثات النقل."},{"n":33,"s":"The word 'intuitively' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["based on careful research","in a confusing way","based on immediate feeling rather than evidence","according to the law"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"It seems obviously true at first.","h":"بديهيًا."},{"n":34,"s":"According to research, where do most of food's greenhouse gas emissions come from?","o":["transport by ship","production, such as fertilizers and livestock","packaging in supermarkets","cooking at home"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"مرحلة الإنتاج."},{"n":35,"s":"Why did British tomatoes produce more emissions than Spanish ones in the study?","o":["They were often grown in heated greenhouses.","They travelled further.","They were transported by air.","They were larger."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"البيوت المحمية المدفأة."},{"n":36,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 4?","o":["to give examples that challenge the food-miles idea","to recommend buying only British food","to describe how lamb is cooked","to explain how ships work"],"k":0,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"The examples contradict simple assumptions.","h":"أمثلة تتحدى الفكرة."},{"n":37,"s":"Which form of transport produces the most emissions per kilogram of food?","o":["shipping by sea","air freight","lorries","trains"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 states this.","h":"الشحن الجوي."},{"n":38,"s":"Why does the writer mention a car trip to the shops?","o":["to encourage people to buy cars","to show that shops are too far away","to explain how supermarkets choose suppliers","to show that the last stage of a journey can be significant"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"It may produce more emissions per item.","h":"أهمية المرحلة الأخيرة."},{"n":39,"s":"What can be inferred about local food production in the Gulf?","o":["It is impossible because of the climate.","It produces more emissions than imports.","It can be valuable for reasons other than transport emissions.","It is not supported by governments."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"It improves food security.","h":"قيمة للأمن الغذائي."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Only local food should be eaten.","Food transport has no environmental impact.","Air freight should be increased.","Simple rules about distance can be misleading; the whole food system matters."],"k":3,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the final paragraph.","h":"القواعد البسيطة مضللة."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Such ______ the demand for tickets that the concert sold out in minutes.","o":["is being","was","were","had"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was — Such + be + noun phrase: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Such."},{"n":42,"s":"If she weren't afraid of flying, she ______ to Japan with us last summer.","o":["would have come","would come","will come","had come"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would have come — Mixed conditional: present state, past result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ in a hurry, she forgot to lock the door.","o":["Been","Being","To be","Having being"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Being — Present participle clause for reason.","h":"Being للسبب."},{"n":44,"s":"It is vital that the patient ______ this medicine twice a day.","o":["takes","taking","will take","take"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"take — Subjunctive after It is vital that.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":45,"s":"There are said ______ more than two hundred species of birds in the reserve.","o":["being","that are","to been","to be"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to be — There are said to be.","h":"There are said to be."},{"n":46,"s":"I'd rather you ______ anything to the others about the surprise yet.","o":["don't say","didn't say","won't say","not say"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"didn't say — would rather + subject + past.","h":"would rather + الماضي."},{"n":47,"s":"What the committee needs ______ more time to study the proposal.","o":["is","are","it is","being"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is — Cleft with What: singular verb.","h":"What … is."},{"n":48,"s":"Only by cooperating with other countries ______ hope to protect the oceans.","o":["we can","we could to","can we","do we can"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"can we — Only by … at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Only by."},{"n":49,"s":"The hotel has two restaurants, both of ______ serve traditional dishes.","o":["whom","them","which","that"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — both of which for things.","h":"both of which."},{"n":50,"s":"______ it not been for your advice, I would have made a terrible mistake.","o":["Had","If","Were","Should"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Had — Had it not been for = if it hadn't been for.","h":"Had it not been for."},{"n":51,"s":"She was exhausted because she ______ all night to finish the project.","o":["has worked","was worked","works","had been working"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been working — Past perfect continuous for cause.","h":"الماضي التام المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"The manager can't stand ______ waiting for meetings to start.","o":["keeping","being kept","to keep","be kept"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"being kept — can't stand + passive gerund.","h":"can't stand + being."},{"n":53,"s":"The two companies have finally agreed ______ the terms of the contract.","o":["at","with","for","on"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"on — agree on something.","h":"agree on."},{"n":54,"s":"You can stay at our house ______ you don't mind sleeping on the sofa.","o":["provided","unless","even if","whereas"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"provided — provided = on condition that.","h":"provided."},{"n":55,"s":"He speaks to his employees as though they ______ children.","o":["were","are being","will be","have been"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — as though + past for unreal comparison.","h":"as though."},{"n":56,"s":"The missing documents ______ in a cupboard in the old office.","o":["eventually found","were eventually finding","were eventually found","had eventually find"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were eventually found — Past simple passive.","h":"الماضي المبني للمجهول."},{"n":57,"s":"______ she lacked experience, she was very confident in the interview.","o":["Despite","In spite of","However","Although"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Although — Although + clause.","h":"Although."},{"n":58,"s":"The officer ordered the crowd ______ back from the barrier.","o":["moving","move","to move","that move"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to move — order + object + to-infinitive.","h":"order sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The older the building, ______ it is to maintain.","o":["more expensive","the most expensive","the more expensive","the expensiver"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the more expensive — Double comparative.","h":"the … the."},{"n":60,"s":"It's no use ______ about the weather; we can't change it.","o":["to complain","complaining","complain","complained"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"complaining — It's no use + -ing.","h":"It's no use + -ing."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"(A) Less people (B) attended the meeting (C) than we (D) expected.","o":["Less","attended","than","expected"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Less — people is countable: Fewer.","h":"Fewer مع المعدود."},{"n":62,"s":"The (A) report (B) must (C) be finish (D) by Friday.","o":["report","must","be finish","by"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"be finish — Modal passive: be finished.","h":"be finished."},{"n":63,"s":"We (A) had (B) a wonderful time (C) at the beach (D) in last summer.","o":["had","a","at","in last"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"in last — No preposition before 'last summer'.","h":"last summer بدون in."},{"n":64,"s":"The (A) students (B) was told (C) to arrive (D) early.","o":["students","was","to","early"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"was — Plural subject: were.","h":"الفاعل جمع."},{"n":65,"s":"(A) If it (B) had not rained, we (C) would go to the (D) park.","o":["If","had","would go","park"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"would go — Third conditional: would have gone.","h":"would have gone."},{"n":66,"s":"(A) Its important (B) to (C) drink enough (D) water in summer.","o":["Its","to","drink","water"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Its — It is = It's.","h":"It's."},{"n":67,"s":"He (A) can (B) to speak (C) three (D) languages.","o":["can","to","three","languages"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to — can + base verb (no 'to').","h":"can بدون to."},{"n":68,"s":"The new (A) library, (B) which (C) opened last month, is very (D) popularly.","o":["library","which","opened","popularly"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"popularly — An adjective is needed after 'is': popular.","h":"الصفة popular."},{"n":69,"s":"She (A) speaks English (B) very (C) good for (D) her age.","o":["speaks","very","good","her"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"good — An adverb is needed: well.","h":"well."},{"n":70,"s":"(A) Who (B) car (C) is parked (D) outside the gate?","o":["Who","car","is","outside"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Who — Possession: Whose.","h":"Whose للملكية."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M27-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What is the first man suggesting?","o":["He wants to buy new pans.","He is too busy to cook.","The fridge is broken.","His flatmate should clean the kitchen."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"He describes the mess.","h":"تلميح لتنظيف المطبخ."}],"t":"A hint about an untidy shared kitchen","sc":["Situation: Two flatmates are talking in the kitchen. (64 words)","Man: I was going to cook dinner tonight, but I couldn't find a single clean pan.","Man: Oh, those are mine in the sink. I've been really busy with assignments this week.","Man: I understand, but there's also a pile of plates on the table and some old food in the fridge. It's starting to smell.","Man: Sorry. I'll clean everything up right now, before you start cooking."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M27-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"How does the second woman feel?","o":["extremely happy","slightly nervous","disappointed","confused"],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"'over the moon' means very happy.","h":"سعيدة جدًا."}],"t":"A friend who is over the moon","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking on the phone. (50 words)","Woman: You sound very cheerful today. What's happened?","Woman: I've just heard that my short story won first prize in the national writing competition! I'm over the moon.","Woman: That's wonderful news! Congratulations!","Woman: Thank you. It's going to be published in a collection next year, and there's a ceremony in Riyadh next month."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M27-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What does the second man mean by 'on the fence'?","o":["He has not decided yet.","He strongly supports the policy.","He strongly opposes the policy.","He will not attend the meeting."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He lists advantages and worries.","h":"لم يقرر بعد."}],"t":"A colleague who is on the fence","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are discussing a proposal. (58 words)","Man: So, are you going to vote for the new flexible working policy at the meeting?","Man: I'm still on the fence. Working from home would save me time, but I'm worried that the team might lose contact.","Man: The proposal includes two office days a week for team meetings.","Man: That helps. I'll read the details again tonight before I decide."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M27-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why does the first man mention the book?","o":["He wants to lend another book.","He wants to know if Tariq liked it.","He wants it back.","He has lost his own copy."],"k":2,"ty":"function","e":"He needs it for his exam.","h":"يريد استرجاعه."}],"t":"A polite reminder about a loan","sc":["Situation: Two friends meet at university. (57 words)","Man: Hi, Tariq. By the way, did you manage to find that book you borrowed from me? The one about economics?","Man: Oh, I'm so sorry. I completely forgot. It's been two months, hasn't it?","Man: It has. I wouldn't mention it, but I need it for my exam next week.","Man: Of course. I'll bring it tomorrow morning, I promise."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M27-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What does the manager really think of the design?","o":["It is exactly what she wanted.","It is too colourful for the customers.","It is too simple.","It is too expensive."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"She suggests something calmer.","h":"ألوانه كثيرة."}],"t":"An implied criticism of a design","sc":["Situation: A manager looks at a designer's work. (55 words)","Woman: Thank you for the new website design. It's certainly very colourful.","Woman: I wanted it to be eye-catching. Do you like it?","Woman: Well, our customers are mostly older professionals. I wonder whether they might prefer something a little calmer and easier to read.","Woman: I see what you mean. I'll prepare a simpler version with fewer colours."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M27-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"What does Ahmed do when he is asked about his salary?","o":["He tells his exact salary.","He becomes angry.","He asks the same question back.","He avoids the question by changing the subject."],"k":3,"ty":"function","e":"He moves to talking about food.","h":"يغير الموضوع بلباقة."}],"t":"A tactful change of topic","sc":["Situation: Three relatives are talking at a family gathering. (56 words)","Man: So, Ahmed, how much do you earn in your new job?","Man: Oh, it's a decent salary. Anyway, have you tried Aunt Sara's mandi? It's the best I've ever tasted.","Man: Not yet. Is it really that good?","Man: Absolutely. She cooks the lamb for hours over a slow fire. Come on, let's get some before it's all gone."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M27-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What is the teacher suggesting?","o":["The clock on the wall is broken.","The student should not keep her phone during the exam.","The student is late for the exam.","The student can use her phone for the time."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"She reminds her of the rules.","h":"تلميح لترك الهاتف."}],"t":"A teacher's hint about cheating rules","sc":["Situation: A teacher speaks to a student before an exam. (57 words)","Woman: Nawal, I notice you've brought your phone into the exam room.","Woman: Yes, I just use it to check the time.","Woman: There's a clock on the wall for that. And you'll remember that any student found with a phone during an exam may have their paper cancelled.","Woman: Oh, of course. I'll leave it with you until the end."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M27-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"How does the second man feel about moving abroad?","o":["only excited","only sad","both excited and worried","completely uninterested Longer conversations"],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"'I am, in a way … But …'.","h":"مشاعر مختلطة."}],"t":"Mixed feelings about moving abroad","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking. (70 words)","Man: So you're moving to Canada for your master's degree next month? You must be excited.","Man: I am, in a way. It's a great university, and I'll learn so much. But I'll miss my family terribly, and I've never lived in such a cold climate.","Man: You'll be able to visit in the holidays, though.","Man: True, and I can call them every day. I suppose it'll be easier once I settle in."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M27-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What is the second woman's main condition for supporting the ban?","o":["Water refill stations must be installed first.","Students must pay for bottles.","The ban must start immediately.","Only staff should follow the ban."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'The ban should only start after … refill stations'.","h":"تركيب محطات التعبئة أولًا."},{"n":80,"s":"Why do they suggest a trial period?","o":["to delay the ban forever","to see whether the plan works before applying it everywhere","to save money on bottles","to punish students who use plastic"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"A trial would show whether the stations are enough.","h":"اختبار الخطة."}],"t":"Discussing a policy proposal for a campus","sc":["Situation: Two members of the student council are talking. (175 words)","Woman: The university wants our opinion on a proposal to ban single-use plastic bottles on campus. What do you think?","Woman: In principle, I support it. The campus produces thousands of plastic bottles every week, and most aren't recycled.","Woman: Agreed, but how would students get drinking water? It's very hot here for most of the year.","Woman: That's the key issue. The ban should only start after the university installs water refill stations in every building.","Woman: And what about cost? Not every student can afford an expensive reusable bottle.","Woman: The university could give a free bottle to every new student. Some universities have already done that, and it worked well.","Woman: We should also suggest a trial period. Perhaps start in two faculties for one semester and see how it goes.","Woman: Good idea. A trial would show whether the refill stations are enough and how students react.","Woman: So our response is: support the ban, but only with refill stations, free bottles and a trial period first.","Woman: Exactly. Let's write that up and send it to the administration by Thursday."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M27-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What does the second man say is most important in the translation?","o":["that every rhyme is kept exactly","that the illustrations are changed","that difficult vocabulary is used","that the Arabic sounds natural when read aloud"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'What matters is that the Arabic sounds natural'.","h":"طبيعية اللغة العربية."},{"n":82,"s":"Why will they test the draft with children?","o":["to find out which words are too difficult","to sell copies of the book","to choose the illustrations","to get permission from the publisher"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"The teacher could identify difficult words.","h":"معرفة الكلمات الصعبة."}],"t":"Planning a translation project","sc":["Situation: Two translation students are planning a project. (183 words)","Man: Our final project is to translate a children's book from English into Arabic. Which one should we choose?","Man: I'd like to choose a picture book about space exploration. It's educational, and there aren't many good Arabic books on the topic for children.","Man: Good choice. But picture books are tricky. The text has to fit the illustrations, and some pages have rhymes.","Man: We don't need to keep the rhymes exactly. What matters is that the Arabic sounds natural and enjoyable when read aloud.","Man: True. And we need to think about the reading level. Should we use Modern Standard Arabic throughout?","Man: Yes, but simple vocabulary and short sentences. Perhaps we could test our draft with some children at my younger brother's school.","Man: That's a great idea. Their teacher could tell us which words are too difficult.","Man: We also need to check copyright. Our professor said we should contact the publisher for permission, even for a student project.","Man: I'll write to the publisher today. Let's divide the book: I'll translate the first half, and you take the second.","Man: Then we'll swap and edit each other's work."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M27-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What is the student's research question about?","o":["the effect of diet on memory","how students choose their courses","the link between sleep and exam scores","the number of hours students study"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"She describes the comparison.","h":"النوم والدرجات."},{"n":84,"s":"Why does the man say she should mention other factors?","o":["Her groups are too large.","Her software is not reliable.","Her professor requires a longer report.","Her results cannot prove that sleep causes higher scores. Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'Correlation doesn't prove causation.'","h":"الارتباط لا يثبت السببية."}],"t":"Choosing a statistical test","sc":["Situation: A student asks a classmate for advice. (170 words)","Woman: I've collected the data for my psychology project, but I don't know which statistical test to use.","Man: What's your research question?","Woman: Whether students who sleep at least seven hours get higher exam scores than students who sleep less.","Man: So you're comparing the average scores of two separate groups. A t-test would usually be appropriate for that.","Woman: That's what I thought. But I only have eighteen students in one group and forty in the other. Is that a problem?","Man: Unequal group sizes aren't necessarily a problem, but you should check whether the scores are roughly normally distributed. If they're very skewed, a different test might be better.","Woman: How do I check that?","Man: You can make a histogram of each group's scores, or use the normality test in the statistics software. Dr. Khalid also has office hours on Wednesday if you're unsure.","Woman: And should I mention that sleep might not be the only factor? Some students may just study more.","Man: Definitely. Correlation doesn't prove causation. Mention it clearly in your limitations section."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M27-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"What does the city wall suggest about Thaj?","o":["It was frequently destroyed by floods.","It was built by Alexander the Great.","It had very few inhabitants.","It was wealthy and needed strong defences."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"الثراء والحاجة للحماية."},{"n":86,"s":"Why was Thaj's location important?","o":["It was on the coast with a large port.","It lay on trade routes and had fresh water.","It was surrounded by high mountains.","It was the capital of Mesopotamia."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Explained in the lecture.","h":"طرق التجارة والماء."},{"n":87,"s":"What was discovered in 1998?","o":["the location of Gerrha","the first Hasaean inscription","a girl's tomb containing a golden mask and jewellery","a Greek temple"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Described in the lecture.","h":"قبر الفتاة والقناع الذهبي."}],"t":"Extended lecture: the archaeology of Thaj","sc":["Situation: An archaeology lecturer speaks to students. (288 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today, we'll look at one of the most important archaeological sites in eastern Saudi Arabia: the ancient city of Thaj, located about ninety kilometres inland from the Gulf coast, near Jubail. Thaj flourished mainly between the fourth century BCE and the first or second century CE, during what archaeologists call the Hellenistic period, when the influence of Greek culture spread across much of the Middle East after the conquests of Alexander the Great. The city was surrounded by a massive stone wall, more than two kilometres long, with towers at regular intervals, which suggests that it was wealthy enough to need, and to afford, strong defences. Why was Thaj important? Its location provides the answer. It lay on trade routes connecting southern Arabia, the Gulf ports and Mesopotamia, and it had access to fresh water, which was essential for caravans crossing the desert. Many scholars believe Thaj may have been closely connected with Gerrha, a wealthy trading city mentioned by Greek and Roman writers, whose exact location has never been confirmed. Excavations at Thaj have revealed coins, pottery, jewellery and inscriptions in an ancient South Arabian script known as Hasaean. One of the most spectacular finds came in 1998, when archaeologists discovered the tomb of a young girl, buried with a golden mask, gold jewellery and precious stones. These objects, now displayed in the National Museum in Riyadh, show the high level of craftsmanship and the wealth of the community. Thaj also shows how Arabia was connected with the wider ancient world. Coins found there imitate those of Alexander the Great, while pottery shows links with Mesopotamia and the Mediterranean. Recent research by Saudi and international teams continues to uncover new information about this remarkable city."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M27-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"According to the lecturer, what is the main challenge for the electricity grid?","o":["the total amount of electricity needed","the timing of charging, which could create peaks in demand","the lack of electric car factories","the high cost of petrol"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'The bigger challenge is not total demand but timing.'","h":"توقيت الشحن."},{"n":89,"s":"What is smart charging?","o":["charging cars as quickly as possible","charging cars only at petrol stations","charging cars automatically when demand is low or renewable energy is plentiful","using solar panels on car roofs"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Explained in the lecture.","h":"الشحن الذكي."},{"n":90,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention that cars are parked most of the day?","o":["to explain how their batteries could store electricity for the grid","to show that people rarely drive","to argue that cars are unnecessary","to explain why parking is expensive"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"Vehicle-to-grid technology.","h":"تخزين الكهرباء في البطاريات."}],"t":"Lecture: how electric cars change energy demand","sc":["Situation: An energy engineering lecturer speaks to students. (297 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Electric vehicles are spreading rapidly in many countries, and Saudi Arabia has announced plans to increase their use significantly in its major cities. Today, I want to discuss how this shift affects energy systems. The first and most obvious effect is that demand moves from petrol stations to the electricity grid. If millions of cars are charged from the grid, total electricity demand will rise. However, the increase is often smaller than people expect, because electric motors are very efficient, converting most of their energy into movement, whereas petrol engines waste much of their energy as heat. The bigger challenge is not total demand but timing. If many drivers return home in the early evening and plug in their cars at the same time, they could create a sharp peak in demand, just when household electricity use is already high. Meeting this peak would require extra power stations that remain idle most of the time, which is expensive. One solution is smart charging. Using software, cars can be charged automatically later at night, when demand is low, or when renewable energy, such as solar or wind power, is plentiful. Electricity companies can encourage this by offering lower prices at off-peak times. A further possibility is called vehicle-to-grid technology. Since most cars are parked for more than ninety per cent of the day, their batteries could store electricity and return some of it to the grid when demand is high, acting like a huge, distributed storage system. This could help balance the variable output of solar power. Finally, we must consider where the electricity comes from. Electric cars produce no exhaust emissions, but their overall environmental benefit depends on how the electricity is generated. As grids include more renewable energy, the advantages of electric vehicles grow."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Arabic calligraphy is a highly respected art form. (2) Calligraphers spend years mastering different scripts. (3) The art is used to decorate mosques, books and public buildings. (4) Many people learn to type on computers at school. (5) Today, young artists are combining calligraphy with modern design."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Typing on computers does not develop the point about calligraphy.","h":"الكتابة بالحاسوب خارج الفكرة."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Children's books are often colourful.","Parents should therefore make reading together a daily habit.","Some parents work long hours.","Libraries close at weekends."],"x":["Reading to young children has many benefits. It builds their vocabulary, develops their imagination and strengthens the bond between parent and child. It also helps children to associate books with pleasure. ______"],"k":1,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It draws a practical conclusion.","h":"خلاصة عملية."},{"n":93,"s":"The (A) manager (B) suggested that we (C) should (D) hiring more staff.","o":["manager","suggested","should","hiring"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"should + base verb: hire.","h":"should + المصدر."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Camels were essential to life in the Arabian desert. (2) They carried goods and people across long distances. (3) They provided milk, meat and wool. (4) Horses are often used in racing. (5) Their ability to survive without water for days made them ideal desert animals."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Horse racing does not develop the point about camels.","h":"سباق الخيل خارج الفكرة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Companies may need to balance open spaces with quiet areas for focused work.","Offices are usually painted white.","Some workers drink tea.","Open-plan offices were popular in the 1960s."],"x":["Many employees now work in open-plan offices. These offices encourage communication and save space. However, they can also be noisy, and many workers find it difficult to concentrate without privacy. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A balanced conclusion.","h":"خلاصة متوازنة."},{"n":96,"s":"She (A) is (B) interesting in studying (C) marine (D) biology.","o":["is","interesting","marine","biology"],"k":1,"ty":"error","e":"Feeling: interested.","h":"interested."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Sport teaches children valuable life skills.","Many sports are played outdoors.","Football is popular worldwide.","Some children dislike exercise."],"x":["______ Children who play sports learn to work in teams, follow rules and cope with losing. They also develop discipline and self-confidence."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe skills learned through sport.","h":"الرياضة تعلم مهارات الحياة."},{"n":98,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) Finally, the finished bricks are stacked and left to dry in the sun.","(2) Traditional mud bricks are made from clay, water and straw.","(3) First, the ingredients are mixed thoroughly by hand or by foot.","(4) Then, the mixture is pressed into wooden moulds."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"materials → First → Then → Finally.","h":"ترتيب الخطوات."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some people are very tall.","Intelligence tests are often long.","However, research shows that abilities can improve greatly with practice and learning.","Many schools have libraries."],"x":["Many people believe that intelligence is fixed at birth. ______ This suggests that effort and good teaching can make a significant difference."],"k":2,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"Contrasting evidence is needed.","h":"دليل مخالف."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["However","For instance","As a result","In contrast Teacher Section — Mock 27"],"x":["The flight was delayed for three hours. ______, the passengers missed their connecting flights."],"k":2,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence is a result.","h":"As a result للنتيجة."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
